/-!
# Effective Cartier divisors on algebraic spaces, read on étale scheme charts

Stacks 083C characterizes the effective Cartier divisors of an algebraic space `X` by their
inverse images on scheme charts: a closed subspace `D ⊆ X` is an effective Cartier divisor
exactly when `D ×_X U` is one on some surjective étale scheme chart `U`, equivalently on every
étale scheme chart, equivalently when every point of `|D|` has an affine étale neighbourhood on
which `D` is cut out by a nonzerodivisor.

This file states those four conditions and proves what is available without the two missing
pieces of infrastructure recorded under "What is not proved" below.  The substance of what *is*
proved is `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_eq_iff_exists_closedSubschemePresentation`: the inverse
image `D ×_X U` on a chart has exactly one closed-subscheme presentation, namely the ideal sheaf
`ker(𝒪_U ⟶ 𝒪_{D ×_X U})` of the canonical base change.  That turns the existentially bundled
chart conditions of Stacks 083C into conditions on a single ideal sheaf of a scheme, which is
what makes the residual gap a statement about *one* named object rather than about a
presentation.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsSchemeEffectiveCartierPullback` identifies the inverse image of a
  closed subspace on a scheme chart with the closed subscheme defined by an effective Cartier
  ideal sheaf; it is the condition "`D ×_X U` is an effective Cartier divisor on `U`" appearing
  in conditions (2) and (3) of Stacks 083C.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HasEffectiveCartierEtaleCover` is condition (2) and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HasEffectiveCartierEtalePullbacks` is condition (3).
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.HasAffinePrincipalEtaleNeighborhoods` is condition (4).

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_eq_iff_exists_closedSubschemePresentation`: an ideal sheaf `I` of
  a scheme chart `U` presents the inverse image `D ×_X U` as a closed subscheme if and only if
  `I` is the kernel of pullback of regular functions along the canonical base change
  `D ×_X U ⟶ U`.  Both directions are extracted from the subfunctor equality that the
  presentation requires: the closed subscheme it names and the canonical base change factor
  through each other, so their kernels agree.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.isSchemeEffectiveCartierPullback_iff`: consequently the chart
  condition of Stacks 083C (2), (3) is exactly effective Cartier-ness of that one kernel.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.hasAffinePrincipalEtaleNeighborhoods_iff`: likewise condition (4) is
  exactly the assertion that this kernel is generated by a nonzerodivisor on the global
  sections of the affine chart.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.ker_rep_snd_eq_ofIdealTop_definingIdealAt`: on an *affine* chart the
  kernel in question is the ideal sheaf of the global-sections ideal
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.definingIdealAt`, so the ideal sheaf of Stacks 083B and the chart
  ideal sheaves of Stacks 083C are the same data.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.hasEffectiveCartierEtaleCover_of_hasEffectiveCartierEtalePullbacks`:
  condition (3) implies condition (2), since an algebraic space has a surjective étale scheme
  chart.

## What is not proved

None of the three equivalences of Stacks 083C is obtained, and in each case the obstruction is
a named result that this library does not have.

* **(2) ⟹ (3), the descent direction.** Passing from one surjective étale chart to every étale
  chart needs invertibility of an ideal sheaf of a scheme to descend along an étale surjection.
  The descent step is `Stacks.InvertibleLocallyFree.InvertibleIsLocal` in
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/InvertibleLocallyFree/Basic.lean`, which is carried there as
  an undischarged hypothesis; it is the descent clause of Stacks 0B8M.  So condition (3) is
  stated and condition (2) is stated, and only the implication is missing.
* **(1) ⟺ (2) and (3) ⟺ (4).** Both compare the ideal sheaf `𝓘_D` on the small étale site of
  `X` with ideal sheaves of schemes.  For (1) ⟺ (2) the comparison needed is the identification
  of modules on the small étale site of a scheme with modules on the scheme, Stacks 03DX(2) and
  03LX; it is absent, and is carried as the hypothesis
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Space.IsCartesianOnAffineCharts` in
  `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/ChartSections.lean`, whose implementation notes record
  that it is blocked on Stacks 03DW.  For (3) ⟺ (4) the missing result is Stacks 01WS, the
  affine-local characterization of an effective Cartier divisor of a *scheme* by a
  nonzerodivisor, which `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Divisors/EffectiveCartier.lean` declines
  because both of its directions pass through "an invertible sheaf of modules is locally free of
  rank one" — again the descent clause of Stacks 0B8M.

`ker_eq_iff_exists_closedSubschemePresentation` reduces all three to statements about the single
ideal sheaf `(D ×_X U ⟶ U).ker`, so no part of the residue is about the presentation data.

## Implementation notes

The canonical base change of a closed subspace `D` along a chart `g : h_U ⟶ X` is supplied by
relative representability of its inclusion: `D.2.2.rep.pullback g` is the representing object,
`D.2.2.rep.snd g` the projection to `U`, and `D.2.2.property_snd g` says that the projection is
a closed immersion of schemes.  That the subfunctor `D.preimage g` is its range is
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.range_yoneda_snd_eq_preimage`.

`IsSchemeEffectiveCartierPullback` is phrased with the presentation data (an ideal sheaf, an
object of the site representing the closed subscheme, and an isomorphism) rather than with the
kernel, because the inverse image is a subfunctor of `h_U` and the site need not contain
`I.subscheme` itself; the isomorphism `e` is what connects the chosen representing object to
Mathlib's `Scheme.IdealSheafData.subscheme`.  The equivalence with the kernel formulation is
`ker_eq_iff_exists_closedSubschemePresentation`, and after it the data play no further role.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 083C](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/083C)
